{"parentUuid":"011f1f87-0610-435b-9d0e-a45244a2be42","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"02e34832-47b7-4b9e-aefa-152f431005be","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017XpnB8PTH9GBXeHHiUoYTP","type":"tool_result","content":"6869://#region src/SplatEdit.ts\n6899:\t\tsuper(), this.isSplatEditSdf = !0;\n6905:\treturn e.isSplatEditSdf;\n6913:\t\tsuper(), this.isSplatEdit = !0, this.rgbaBlendMode = r, this.sdfSmooth = i, this.softEdge = a, this.invert = o, this.sdfs = s, this.ordering = t.nextOrdering++, th\n6923:\treturn e.isSplatEdit;\n10922:export { Pm as DEFAULT_CONTROLLER_FAST_MULTIPLIER, zm as DEFAULT_CONTROLLER_GETFAST, Lm as DEFAULT_CONTROLLER_GETMOVE, Rm as DEFAULT_CONTROLLER_GETROTATE, Bm as DEF\n10922:export { Pm as DEFAULT_CONTROLLER_FAST_MULTIPLIER, zm as DEFAULT_CONTROLLE\n $f as SplatEdit\n Yf as SplatEditRgbaBlendMode\n Zf as SplatEditSdf\n qf as SplatEditSdfType\n tp as SplatEdits\n\t\t\tthis.context.viewToObject.updateFromMatrix(n) && (this.enableViewToObject || this.context.splats.hasRgbDir()) && (d = !0);\n\t\t}\n\t\tlet p = new e.Vector4(this.recolor.r, this.recolor.g, this.recolor.b, this.opacity);\n\t\tp.equals(this.context.recolor.value) || (this.context.recolor.value.copy(p), d = !0);\n\t\tlet m = this.editable ? (this.edits ?? []).concat(c) : [];\n\t\tthis.editable && !this.edits && this.traverseVisible((e) => {\n\t\t\tep(e) && m.push(e);\n\t\t}), m.sort((e, t) => e.ordering - t.ordering);\n\t\tlet h = m.map((e) => {\n\t\t\tif (e.sdfs != null) return {\n\t\t\t\tedit: e,\n=== scan-to-floor-plan-dxf\nREADME.md 7174;images 0;requirements.txt 53;run.bat 1320;sweeps.py 5234;tests 0;visualize.py 4114;walls_to_vectors.py 38880;\n\n# Scan to floor plan, from wall pixels to a DXF\nTurn the wall points of a classified room scan into clean vector walls and a layered DXF any CAD tool opens. CPU only.\n![A classroom's walls as grid pixels beside the same walls as four vector lines](images/01-pixels-to-lines.webp)\nLeft, 3,275 wall pixels. Right, four lines with eight endpoints and a 55.42 m2 room: the jump this script makes.\n  Get the dataset · Read the article · More tutorials\n  · The book\n---\n## What it does\nReads a classified classroom scan (1,422,440 points), levels it on a plane fitted to every floor point, keeps the band of wall points between floor and ceiling and rasterizes it on a 2 cm grid. Then it does the\n![Four vector walls standing over the classified classroom scan, one in orange](images/02-walls-in-the-scan.webp)\nThe walls stood back up in the scan. The orange one stands where the band saw 12 % of a wall and the floor strip saw all of it, so the DXF puts it on WALL_ASSUMED.\n## Run it\n```bash\npip install -r requirements.txt\n=== 3d-scene-labeling-gui\nREADME.md 7067;da_semantic_masking.py 26030;images 0;interactive_painting.py 6773;kids_strokes.json 5833;requirements.txt 55;run_headless.py 6688;\n\n# Label a 3D scene by painting on photos\nPaint labels on photos with an OpenCV brush, project them to 3D through the cameras, spread them with a KD-tree, export PLY and GLB. CPU only.\n![A toy set point cloud with sparse painted labels beside the same cloud after label fusion](images/01-painted-vs-fused.webp)\nLeft, what 55 brush strokes reach through the cameras: 11.9 % of the points. Right, after KD-tree fusion: 83.3 %.\n  Get the dataset · Read the article · More tutorials\n  · The book\n---\n## What it does\nOpens each photo in an OpenCV window where you paint up to five classes with a brush. Every painted pixel is back-projected through its camera into the 3D scene and matched to the nearest point within 1 cm; the\n![A photo, the class mask painted on it, and the two overlaid](images/02-painted-mask.webp)\nStep 2: one photo, the mask painted on it, and the overlay that shows where each class landed.\n## Run it\n```bash\npip install -r requirements.txt\n=== open-vocabulary-3d-semantics\nREADME.md 4829;dataviz.py 9980;images 0;open_vocab_3d_semantics.py 36938;requirements.txt 31;\n\n# Open-vocabulary 3D semantics on a real room scan\nLift 2D labels onto a real room scan, fuse them across cameras, vote out the flicker, slice a plan. CPU only; model outputs simulated.\n![The living-room scan in colour beside the same scan labeled by class](images/01-scan-to-labels.webp)\nLeft, the scan as captured. Right, the labels after lifting, fusion, the vote and one click (ceiling cut away).\n  Get the dataset · Read the article · More tutorials\n  · The book\n---\n## What it does\nLoads a real handheld scan of a living room (1 million points, colour and a class on every point), levels it on the floor with RANSAC, scales it from the ceiling, and builds the pixel-to-point link with a depth\n![Share of points agreeing with the scan's reference labels at each stage of the pipeline](images/02-agreement-by-stage.webp)\nMeasured on the scan: fusion across views does the heavy lifting; a plain vote loses ground, a gated one gains it.\n## Run it\n```bash\npip install -r requirements.txt\n=== wall-thickness-to-ifc\nREADME.md 9784;check_determinism.py 3392;images 0;prepare_s3dis.py 15600;requirements.txt 54;run.bat 1223;tests 0;visualize.py 5927;wall_thickness_to_ifc.py 46637;\n\n# Wall thickness from a scan, written to IFC\nFind the two faces of a wall as density peaks, pair them, measure the gap cell by cell, write an IfcWall and prove it. CPU only.\n![Three IFC wall solids read back from the file, standing among the office wall points](images/01-ifc-walls-over-scan.webp)\nThe three IfcWall solids, read back from the written file, standing among the points they were measured from: 13.78, 12.59 and 14.87 cm.\n  Get the reference output · Read the article · More tutorials\n  · The book\n---\n## What it does\nLevels a classified office scan on the bisector of its floor and ceiling planes, finds the two wall directions from the normals, and projects the wall points onto each normal so every face becomes a peak in a 1\n![A wall edge-on with one face leaning and one standing, beside every matched cell coloured by its thickness](images/02-thickness-at-mid-height.webp)\nLeft, the challenge wall edge-on: one face leans, one stands, 14.87 cm at mid-height. Right, all 150 matched cells, each coloured by the thickness measured in it.\n## Get the data\nThis kit does not ship the scan, because its terms forbid passing it on. Get it\nfrom the official page: **[S3DIS, the Stanford Large-Scale 3D Indoor Spaces dataset](http://buildingparser.stanford.edu/dataset.html)**.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"ac33dfc5-955c-412f-a05d-b55efcd3dacb","timestamp":"2026-11-07T11:39:26.558Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"6869://#region src/SplatEdit.ts\n6899:\t\tsuper(), this.isSplatEditSdf = !0;\n6905:\treturn e.isSplatEditSdf;\n6913:\t\tsuper(), this.isSplatEdit = !0, this.rgbaBlendMode = r, this.sdfSmooth = i, this.softEdge = a, this.invert = o, this.sdfs = s, this.ordering = t.nextOrdering++, th\n6923:\treturn e.isSplatEdit;\n10922:export { Pm as DEFAULT_CONTROLLER_FAST_MULTIPLIER, zm as DEFAULT_CONTROLLER_GETFAST, Lm as DEFAULT_CONTROLLER_GETMOVE, Rm as DEFAULT_CONTROLLER_GETROTATE, Bm as DEF\n10922:export { Pm as DEFAULT_CONTROLLER_FAST_MULTIPLIER, zm as DEFAULT_CONTROLLE\n $f as SplatEdit\n Yf as SplatEditRgbaBlendMode\n Zf as SplatEditSdf\n qf as SplatEditSdfType\n tp as SplatEdits\n\t\t\tthis.context.viewToObject.updateFromMatrix(n) && (this.enableViewToObject || this.context.splats.hasRgbDir()) && (d = !0);\n\t\t}\n\t\tlet p = new e.Vector4(this.recolor.r, this.recolor.g, this.recolor.b, this.opacity);\n\t\tp.equals(this.context.recolor.value) || (this.context.recolor.value.copy(p), d = !0);\n\t\tlet m = this.editable ? (this.edits ?? []).concat(c) : [];\n\t\tthis.editable && !this.edits && this.traverseVisible((e) => {\n\t\t\tep(e) && m.push(e);\n\t\t}), m.sort((e, t) => e.ordering - t.ordering);\n\t\tlet h = m.map((e) => {\n\t\t\tif (e.sdfs != null) return {\n\t\t\t\tedit: e,\n=== scan-to-floor-plan-dxf\nREADME.md 7174;images 0;requirements.txt 53;run.bat 1320;sweeps.py 5234;tests 0;visualize.py 4114;walls_to_vectors.py 38880;\n\n# Scan to floor plan, from wall pixels to a DXF\nTurn the wall points of a classified room scan into clean vector walls and a layered DXF any CAD tool opens. CPU only.\n![A classroom's walls as grid pixels beside the same walls as four vector lines](images/01-pixels-to-lines.webp)\nLeft, 3,275 wall pixels. Right, four lines with eight endpoints and a 55.42 m2 room: the jump this script makes.\n  Get the dataset · Read the article · More tutorials\n  · The book\n---\n## What it does\nReads a classified classroom scan (1,422,440 points), levels it on a plane fitted to every floor point, keeps the band of wall points between floor and ceiling and rasterizes it on a 2 cm grid. Then it does the\n![Four vector walls standing over the classified classroom scan, one in orange](images/02-walls-in-the-scan.webp)\nThe walls stood back up in the scan. The orange one stands where the band saw 12 % of a wall and the floor strip saw all of it, so the DXF puts it on WALL_ASSUMED.\n## Run it\n```bash\npip install -r requirements.txt\n=== 3d-scene-labeling-gui\nREADME.md 7067;da_semantic_masking.py 26030;images 0;interactive_painting.py 6773;kids_strokes.json 5833;requirements.txt 55;run_headless.py 6688;\n\n# Label a 3D scene by painting on photos\nPaint labels on photos with an OpenCV brush, project them to 3D through the cameras, spread them with a KD-tree, export PLY and GLB. CPU only.\n![A toy set point cloud with sparse painted labels beside the same cloud after label fusion](images/01-painted-vs-fused.webp)\nLeft, what 55 brush strokes reach through the cameras: 11.9 % of the points. Right, after KD-tree fusion: 83.3 %.\n  Get the dataset · Read the article · More tutorials\n  · The book\n---\n## What it does\nOpens each photo in an OpenCV window where you paint up to five classes with a brush. Every painted pixel is back-projected through its camera into the 3D scene and matched to the nearest point within 1 cm; the\n![A photo, the class mask painted on it, and the two overlaid](images/02-painted-mask.webp)\nStep 2: one photo, the mask painted on it, and the overlay that shows where each class landed.\n## Run it\n```bash\npip install -r requirements.txt\n=== open-vocabulary-3d-semantics\nREADME.md 4829;dataviz.py 9980;images 0;open_vocab_3d_semantics.py 36938;requirements.txt 31;\n\n# Open-vocabulary 3D semantics on a real room scan\nLift 2D labels onto a real room scan, fuse them across cameras, vote out the flicker, slice a plan. CPU only; model outputs simulated.\n![The living-room scan in colour beside the same scan labeled by class](images/01-scan-to-labels.webp)\nLeft, the scan as captured. Right, the labels after lifting, fusion, the vote and one click (ceiling cut away).\n  Get the dataset · Read the article · More tutorials\n  · The book\n---\n## What it does\nLoads a real handheld scan of a living room (1 million points, colour and a class on every point), levels it on the floor with RANSAC, scales it from the ceiling, and builds the pixel-to-point link with a depth\n![Share of points agreeing with the scan's reference labels at each stage of the pipeline](images/02-agreement-by-stage.webp)\nMeasured on the scan: fusion across views does the heavy lifting; a plain vote loses ground, a gated one gains it.\n## Run it\n```bash\npip install -r requirements.txt\n=== wall-thickness-to-ifc\nREADME.md 9784;check_determinism.py 3392;images 0;prepare_s3dis.py 15600;requirements.txt 54;run.bat 1223;tests 0;visualize.py 5927;wall_thickness_to_ifc.py 46637;\n\n# Wall thickness from a scan, written to IFC\nFind the two faces of a wall as density peaks, pair them, measure the gap cell by cell, write an IfcWall and prove it. CPU only.\n![Three IFC wall solids read back from the file, standing among the office wall points](images/01-ifc-walls-over-scan.webp)\nThe three IfcWall solids, read back from the written file, standing among the points they were measured from: 13.78, 12.59 and 14.87 cm.\n  Get the reference output · Read the article · More tutorials\n  · The book\n---\n## What it does\nLevels a classified office scan on the bisector of its floor and ceiling planes, finds the two wall directions from the normals, and projects the wall points onto each normal so every face becomes a peak in a 1\n![A wall edge-on with one face leaning and one standing, beside every matched cell coloured by its thickness](images/02-thickness-at-mid-height.webp)\nLeft, the challenge wall edge-on: one face leans, one stands, 14.87 cm at mid-height. Right, all 150 matched cells, each coloured by the thickness measured in it.\n## Get the data\nThis kit does not ship the scan, because its terms forbid passing it on. Get it\nfrom the official page: **[S3DIS, the Stanford Large-Scale 3D Indoor Spaces dataset](http://buildingparser.stanford.edu/dataset.html)**.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"33114da2-9bf7-4ca9-af1b-082f91d07d22","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/viktor-trilar/Projects/Newbies","root":"/home/viktor-trilar/Projects/Newbies","branch":"viktor/usage-update","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"Sairanyx/Newbies","visibility":"unknown"},"push_remote":null,"remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"Sairanyx/Newbies","visibility":"unknown"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"Sairanyx/Newbies","visibility":"unknown"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/viktor-trilar/Projects/Newbies","platform":"linux"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"011f1f87-0610-435b-9d0e-a45244a2be42","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/viktor-trilar/Projects